[ ] 12 Let us implement node measuring certain processes something like this
       ssh eth4 "pidstat -p 5365,6902 30 1 2>/dev/null | awk '/Average/ {print}'"
            Average:      UID       PID    %usr %system  %guest   %wait    %CPU   CPU  Command
            Average:     1000      5365  101.63   11.07    0.00    0.00  112.70     -  geth-pulse
            Average:     1000      6902  155.43   14.63    0.00    0.00  170.07     -  geth-pulse

       but the PID should be obtained by checking the pre-set process name(s).

[ ] 13 Add a node to periodically check if a process exists on a remote host,
       report success if so, false if not. Report the PID as "value".

[ ] 15 The functional tests are executed very fast so that the user can't see
       what happens. Let us slow them down a little but. They can finish fast,
       but every step that causes visual update should take about 1 second at
       least.

[ ] 20 Add an on-demand poll mechanism for Tasmota devices that piggybacks on
       the new MQTT sink: a node (or a worksheet recipe documented in the help)
       that publishes "cmnd/tasmotas/Status 5" to the broker and listens on
       "stat/+/STATUS5" through an MQTT source, then surfaces each device's
       IPAddress / Hostname / Mac from the StatusNET reply. The result is a
       discovery feed that can be wired into the HTTPS Client (or a future plain
       HTTP client) to drive per-device REST calls without any external scan.

[ ] 26 The Text to Speech node should default to "any engine you find" + "any
       voice you find": on a fresh node, auto-detect the first available TTS
       engine (piper/espeak-ng/espeak/festival/flite on $PATH) and the first
       available voice for it, instead of defaulting to a fixed engine/model
       that may not exist on the machine. The auto-pick must stay overridable in
       the editor (keep the existing engine/voice selectors). Once this lands,
       update the bundled panel applets -- the shipped starter worksheet
       panel-plugins/pipnode-xfce-applet/ pipnode-xfce-applet-default.json
       currently hard-codes an absolute ~/.local/share/piper/...onnx model path
       -- to rely on the auto-default so a new applet's TTS works on any machine
       out of the box. (Came up while wiring the panel applet's click -> Panel
       Input -> Text to Speech demo: the shipped template only spoke on the
       author's machine.)

[ ] 28 Host-managed shared profile sessions (plugin ABI v6). Today a profile
       (ABI v5, item 27) is a CONFIG store: a node resolves a profile-ref at
       fire time, gets host/port/credentials, and opens its own connection. That
       is fine for one-shot HTTP-style work but breaks down whenever the
       underlying resource is single-owner or stateful: a USB-serial device can
       only be held by one fd, and an MQTT broker session is logically one TCP
       socket per (broker,client-id) that wants its subscriptions and outbound
       publishes funnelled through one place. The Meshtastic node already paid
       this tax (one device <-> one node, no multi-node fan-out possible) and
       the upcoming pipnode-zigbee-plugin would inherit the same limitation (the
       GW70-MQTT CC2652 dongle speaks ZNP over /dev/ttyUSB0; multiple debug
       nodes -- status, list-devices, dump-frames, permit-join -- all need to
       share that one fd, queue requests serially, and fan unsolicited frames
       out to subscribers).

       Fix it at the host. Keep the existing profile as the CONFIG layer, and
       add a LIVE-RESOURCE layer on top: PnProfileSession, a per-profile-id
       object opened on first reference and torn down on last release, owned and
       ref-counted by the host. The plugin teaches the host how to build one via
       two new vfuncs on PnProfileSchemaClass:
           PnProfileSession *(*open_session)  (PnProfile *cfg, GError **error);
           void              (*close_session) (PnProfileSession *session);
       Host-side API the nodes use:
           PnProfileSession *pn_profile_acquire_session (const gchar *profile_id,
                                                         GError **);
           void              pn_profile_release_session (PnProfileSession *);
       Acquire in constructed(), release in dispose(), exactly mirroring the
       existing pn_node_get_profile lifecycle. Re-emit PnVault::changed as a
       session teardown+rebuild so a credential edit propagates the same way it
       does today. State changes (device unplug, broker disconnect) fan out via
       a PnProfileSession::state-changed signal mirroring PnVault::changed.
       Concurrency model: single-threaded GLib mainloop, GIOChannel/GUnixIO on
       the fd, all callbacks on the main thread (no new threads -- pipnode does
       not use them elsewhere). Lives in libpipnode-core so pipnode-run gets the
       same shared sessions headlessly (see item 23).

       The session object itself is plugin-defined. For Zigbee that means a ZNP
       transport with a FIFO request queue (pn_znp_request(session, cmd,
       payload, callback)) plus a subscriber registry for unsolicited frames
       (pn_znp_subscribe(session, type, callback)); for MQTT it is a libmosq
       client with one TCP connection, a subscribe-multiplex table and a publish
       queue; for Meshtastic a serial-mesh session of the same shape as Zigbee.

       Pilot/port order, smallest blast radius first: (a) ship the host-side
       registry + the two vfuncs + the ABI bump 5 -> 6, no plugin work; (b) port
       the bundled MQTT Source/Sink in plugins/network onto it -- the win is
       immediate and observable (N MQTT nodes against one broker stop dialling N
       sockets, and their subscriptions merge into one), and it proves the API
       on existing test coverage; (c) build the Zigbee plugin greenfield against
       the now-proven API (GW70Profile + ZnpSession + small debug nodes -- see
       item 29 if/when filed); (d) retrofit the Meshtastic node to lift its
       one-device-one-node ceiling; (e) opportunistically: the kodi plugin's
       JSON-RPC client (multiple kodi nodes currently each open their own
       libsoup session), shell SSH, any future stateful-socket plugin.

       Trade-offs to nail in design: ownership boundary (plugin defines what a
       session IS, host owns the registry + ref-count + lifetime; plugin never
       caches a PnProfileSession* outside an acquire/release pair), error
       propagation (every subscribed node must learn when the device disappears
       -- state-changed signal), and the headless story (sessions must work
       under pipnode-run, which is why the layer lives in core not gui). Same
       ABI-bump drill as item 27: sudo make install + reinstall every plugin or
       the scan will deadlock on stale .so's (see the caveat documented under
       item 23).

[ ] 29 Devices -> Meshtastic configuration dialog. Today PnMeshtastic nodes are
       unusable until the device is set up via the external /usr/bin/pip-mesh
       bash prototype (4994 lines, hand-rolled bash protobuf, USB serial only,
       channel/QR share tested working with the author's handheld). Port that
       knowledge into a first-class editor dialog so an unboxer can go from
       "plug in a Heltec/Tracker" to "send a test message" without leaving
       pipnode. Constraints from the user: device is the source of truth,
       NOTHING persisted on pipnode's side (close = disconnect, no caches, no
       auto-scan, no "remembered devices"); discovery only on explicit Scan;
       list devices, never auto-select.

       Shape decided in chat: top-level Devices menu in the editor with one
       entry per supported device kind, first being "Meshtastic..."; opens a
       GtkDialog with the device list on the left (empty until Scan), a
       stage-based settings stack on the right (Identity / Region / Channels /
       Share / Test). Per-device session is held only while the dialog is open.
       Code lives in the editor (lib/devices/meshtastic/) for the first cut;
       designed with a clean seam so Phase 8 moves it into the meshtastic plugin
       and turns the menu into a plugin-registered list.

       Architecture decisions: (a) hand-translate pip-mesh's narrow protobuf
       subset into C rather than pull in nanopb -- the surface we write (LoRa,
       owner, channels) is small and self-contained; (b) optional libqrencode
       dep, auto-detected at configure time, URL-only fallback;
       (c) discovery via sysfs walk against pip-mesh's VID:PID table (10c4:ea60
       Heltec V3, 239a:0029/8027/8029 Trackers), no libgudev; (d) threading:
       dialog on main thread, serial I/O on GTask worker threads, results
       marshalled back via g_main_context_invoke -- no blocking the UI, no
       g_idle polling. Defaults chosen for the open questions, all revisitable:
       libqrencode optional dep; transport-profile-save deferred to Phase 8;
       Region page covers the full LoRa surface (not just pip-mesh's QR subset);
       test page is text-only first.

       File layout (flat lib/ to match repo convention; subdir grouping is
       notional, the prefix groups them. Phase 8 may move them under a plugin
       tree but for now keep them next to the other lib/pn-*.c):
           src/pn-devices-menu.{c,h}              -- Devices menu host (editor only)
           lib/pn-mesh-discover.{c,h}             -- sysfs walk, VID:PID table
           lib/pn-mesh-serial.{c,h}               -- termios open, frame I/O
           lib/pn-mesh-pb.{c,h}                   -- minimal protobuf codec
           lib/pn-mesh-protos.{c,h}               -- typed accessors per message
           lib/pn-mesh-connection.{c,h}           -- per-device session, admin frames
           lib/pn-mesh-qr.{c,h}                   -- ChannelSet -> URL -> optional pixmap
           lib/pn-mesh-dialog.{c,h}               -- top-level GtkDialog
           lib/pn-mesh-device-list.{c,h}          -- left: scan button + GtkListBox
           lib/pn-mesh-page-identity.{c,h}        -- metadata + owner names
           lib/pn-mesh-page-region.{c,h}          -- region / preset / hop / tx-power
           lib/pn-mesh-page-channels.{c,h}        -- list/create/delete/copy (txn)
           lib/pn-mesh-page-share.{c,h}           -- QR + URL for channels / full config
           lib/pn-mesh-page-test.{c,h}            -- send + live monitor
           tests/unit/
               test-pn-mesh-pb.c, test-pn-mesh-frame.c, test-pn-mesh-qr.c

