               DONE 2026-05-28: extracted to pn_mqtt_parse_url in
               pn-mqtt-util.c, both nodes call it.
         - [x] N4 pn_mqtt_parse_url mis-parses IPv6 literals: it locates the
               port with strchr(p, ':'), so tcp://[::1]:1883 splits on the first
               ':' inside the address. Bracketed-IPv6 host[:port] is
               unsupported; handle '[...]' before scanning for the port colon
               (then add a parse-table case -- today's behaviour is a bug, so
               don't pin it).
               DONE 2026-05-29: pn_mqtt_parse_url handles a leading '[' — host
               is the bracket contents, an optional ":port" may follow the ']',
               and an unterminated/empty/junk-after form is rejected. Port
               parsing factored into a parse_port_str helper (also requires the
               port token be fully consumed, *endp=='\0'). New test-pn-mqtt-util
               url_ipv6 case covers tcp://[::1], [::1], mqtts://[2001:db8::1],
               ...:port, and the malformed rejections.
         - [x] U1 Inline Broker URL silently ignored once any mqtt-broker
               profile exists: empty broker-profile -> pn_node_get_profile falls
               back to the PRIMARY profile, and pn_mqtt_resolve_connection takes
               every field from a non-NULL profile, so a typed url did nothing
               (user report 2026-05-29: "no matter what I write to Broker URL it
               keeps using the saved broker").
               DONE 2026-05-29 (final model = explicit "Custom settings"): the
               Broker profile picker gained a third choice. broker-profile now
               has three states -- "" = Default/primary profile, "<id>" = that
               profile, and the reserved sentinel PN_PROFILE_REF_CUSTOM
               ("@custom") = "Custom settings", i.e. use the node's own inline
               url/credentials. pn_node_get_profile() returns NULL for the
               sentinel (no primary fallback), so resolution is back to the
               simple profile-XOR-inline (pn_mqtt_resolve_connection reverted
               from the interim per-field variant). Applies to BOTH Source and
               Sink. Opt-in via
               pn_param_spec_set_profile_ref_inline_fields(pspec,
               "url,username,password,client-id") on the shared profile-ref
               combo in pn-node-dialog.c, which adds the "Custom settings" entry
               (LAST in the list).
               SAVE/LOAD INVARIANT + GATING (2026-05-29 follow-up): inline
               custom data exists ONLY in "Custom settings" mode. (a) The combo
               greys out the tagged inline-field editors unless "@custom" is
               selected (find-by-name "pn-prop-<field>" under the grid; applied
               on combo "map" + each populate). (b) Picking Default/a profile
               clears those fields (""). (c) On load, migrate_legacy_credentials
               clears stale inline data when a real profile id is selected,
               keeps it for "@custom", and for "" still does the legacy
               creds-import / url->@custom (a new-model Default node has empty
               inline fields, so that flip only fires for genuinely legacy files
               -- distinguished by url emptiness, no extra flag). This makes the
               picker's choice survive save->load reliably (the reported bug:
               leftover inline url re-triggered Custom on load). The earlier
               "editing url auto-selects Custom" was removed -- a greyed field
               can't be edited; the user picks Custom via the combo. Bundled
               example mqtt-test.json set to "@custom". Covered by
               test-pn-mqtt-resolve.c (profile_wins,
               custom_sentinel_uses_inline); clearing/gating are dialog/idle
               behavior, verified manually. Docs: url property blurb +
               PnMqtt.html / PnMqttSink.html. (History: first "explicit profile
               wins / typed url overrides primary", then per-field, then this
               explicit-mode model + the save/load invariant.)

