         REMAINING (deferred / doc-only, not blocking):
           - [x] M2 DONE 2026-06-07. Decided: clear the backlog on a genuine
                 broker change so commands buffered for broker A are never
                 misdelivered to broker B. restart_client records the resolved
                 broker URL (priv->broker_identity) and compares it on each
                 (re)connect via the new pure helper pn_mqtt_pending_retarget
                 (pn-mqtt-util): a NULL prior identity (first connect, where
                 load-time commands legitimately wait) or an unchanged identity
                 (transient drop / vault touch to the same broker) keeps the
                 queue; a different URL -- including clearing the broker to ""
                 -- drops it and logs the discard count. Covered by
                 test-pn-mqtt-util queue_retarget; documented in
                 PnMqttSink.html.
           - [x] M3 DONE 2026-06-08. The MQTT Broker profile now carries the
                 TLS trust material + keep-alive it lacked. (port-as-int was
                 dropped: pn_mqtt_parse_url already honours host:port in the
                 URL, so an INT port field would only duplicate/contradict it.)
                   [x] Schema (pn-mqtt-profile.c): five new fields after the
                       four identity ones — ca-file / client-cert / client-key
                       (STRING paths, not secrets: filesystem paths the host
                       trusts, stored unmasked), tls-insecure (BOOL, off), and
                       keepalive (INT, schema default "60"). Backward-compatible:
                       unset fields resolve to their defaults, so existing vault
                       profiles keep working unchanged.
                   [x] Resolver seam (pn_mqtt_resolve_connection, pn-mqtt-util):
                       new out-param PnMqttConnOpts {ca_file, client_cert,
                       client_key, tls_insecure, keepalive} + pn_mqtt_conn_opts_clear.
                       TLS/keepalive come ONLY from a profile; inline "Custom
                       settings" mode yields the unset defaults ("" / FALSE / 0).
                       Threaded through BOTH the Source (pn-mqtt.c) and Sink
                       (pn-mqtt-sink.c) wrappers so they cannot diverge.
                   [x] Wiring (both restart_client): cafile/cert/key fed to
                       mosquitto_tls_set (empty -> NULL, keeping today's
                       system-trust default); a lone half of the mTLS pair is
                       rejected with a logged error; tls-insecure ->
                       mosquitto_tls_insecure_set (logged WARNING, never silent);
                       keep-alive via the new pure pn_mqtt_keepalive_seconds()
                       clamp (<=0 -> 60 default, >65535 -> 65535). The two
                       per-node PN_MQTT*_KEEPALIVE_SECONDS #defines are gone.
                   [x] mTLS included (not deferred): client-cert/client-key wired
                       into the same mosquitto_tls_set call.
                   [x] Docs: data/help/MqttBrokerProfile.html TLS section +
                       field table cover CA file / mTLS pair / insecure toggle /
                       keepalive; PnMqtt.html private-PKI note points at the
                       profile fields instead of "install the CA in the OS store".
                   [x] Tests (make check = 97/97): test-pn-mqtt-resolve gained
                       inline_opts_default / profile_tls_opts / profile_opts_defaults;
                       test-pn-mqtt-util gained keepalive_seconds (clamp); the
                       test-pn-mqtt-profile characterization test updated to the
                       9-field schema.
                   NOTE: ABI-clean (no PnNodeClass vfunc change), so no plugin
                   reinstall needed. Not yet exercised against a real private-CA
                   broker (the homelab Mosquitto uses the OS trust store) — the
                   resolve/clamp/validation logic is unit-tested; the live
                   mosquitto_tls_* calls are the only untested-against-hardware
                   part.
           - [x] M4 DONE 2026-06-07. The gsize->int narrowing at both publish
                 sites is now guarded: new pure predicate
                 pn_mqtt_payload_within_limit (pn-mqtt-util, ceiling
                 PN_MQTT_MAX_PAYLOAD_BYTES = 268,435,455) screens the length
                 before the cast. A live publish past the limit returns
                 MOSQ_ERR_PAYLOAD_SIZE via the sink_publish wrapper (surfaced
                 through the existing error path); an oversize payload arriving
                 while offline is dropped at receive with a Log-dialog error
                 instead of buffering a doomed (huge) payload. Covered by
                 test-pn-mqtt-util payload_within_limit; documented in
                 PnMqttSink.html ("What gets dropped").
           - [x] L3 BY DESIGN (closed 2026-06-07). Default topic "#" +
                 primary profile auto-subscribes the whole broker -- wanted,
                 not a footgun: a fresh Source should show the user
                 EVERYTHING on the broker so they can see what is flowing,
                 then narrow. Default "#" stays.
           - [x] L4 INACCURATE (closed 2026-06-07). An empty payload is NOT
                 dropped -- it publishes as a zero-length message, which is
                 exactly how a retained topic is cleared (empty data.payload
                 or empty-resolving template + retain on). Only an ABSENT
                 data.payload member is dropped (deliberate "nothing to send",
                 test_payload_missing_drops). Contract now documented in
                 PnMqttSink.html ("Empty payloads"). See the matching entry
                 in the earlier review block above for the full rationale.

[ ] 33 Meshtastic discovery: stop treating a BARE 10c4:ea60 as a Meshtastic
       device. The VID:PID table in lib/pn-mesh-discover.c (KNOWN_DEVICES)
       matches the Heltec V3 on 10c4:ea60 -- but that is the GENERIC Silicon
       Labs CP2102 /
       CP2102N USB-UART chip, used by countless unrelated boards. In particular
       the common Sonoff/ITead Zigbee dongles (ZBDongle-P) are CP2102N ->
       10c4:ea60, so a plugged-in Zigbee coordinator shows up in the Meshtastic
       dialog as a "Heltec V3". Picking it aims the Meshtastic want_config_id
       handshake at a Z-Stack UART (root cause of the 2026-05-29 zigbee2mqtt
       CPU-spin incident: pipnode opened the shared /dev/ttyUSB and wrote
       Meshtastic frames into it; zigbee2mqtt choked on the corrupted byte
       stream).

         MITIGATED 2026-05-29 (see the in-use guard added the same day): such a
         device, while it is open by zigbee2mqtt (or anything else), now appears
         DISABLED in the list with an "in use by <proc>" note and can never be
         opened (pn_mesh_tty_in_use + TIOCEXCL in pn-mesh-serial.c). But that
         only helps WHILE the other process holds the port -- an idle, unclaimed
         CP2102 Zigbee dongle would still be mislabelled "Heltec V3" and be
         connectable.

         THE WORK (why it is hard): sysfs alone cannot disambiguate -- the
         product / manufacturer / serial strings are the chip's generic defaults
         ("CP2102 USB to UART Bridge Controller" / "Silicon Labs"), identical on
         a Heltec and a Zigbee dongle. The only reliable tell is a safe,
         read-only probe over the wire (e.g. a Meshtastic want_config_id and a
         bounded wait for a valid 0x94 0xC3 frame) -- but ANY write is exactly
         what we must NOT do to a non-Meshtastic device. Options to weigh: (a)
         mark generic-CP2102 matches as "unverified / possibly another device"
         in the UI and require an explicit user confirm before the first write;
         (b) keep a denylist of known-NON-mesh CP2102 product/serial strings;
         (c) cross-check against a system Zigbee/serial registry. None is clean;
         deferred deliberately.

[ ] 36 Stop ModemManager from probing Meshtastic serial devices. When a tty
       appears, ModemManager (root) opens it and fires AT commands -- which
       toggles DTR/RTS (rebooting the ESP32) and races pipnode's open. pipnode's
       in-use guard is a /proc scan that can't see root-owned holders, so it
       misses MM. Fix: ship a udev rule setting ID_MM_DEVICE_IGNORE=1 for the
       Meshtastic VID:PIDs (packaging step), and/or inhibit the device over MM's
       D-Bus API around open. Caveat: 10c4:ea60 is generic CP2102 (see #33); a
       blanket ignore also hides real CP2102 modems. Touches system config --
       weigh against the minimal-invasive-packaging line.

